/* Source-authorised lesson notes transcribed from Unreal Engine Mini-Projects (2026).
   Screenshots and original assets are not bundled in this text data file.
   Page references allow cross-checking with the licensed source PDF. */
window.UE5_MINI_PROJECT_NOTES = {"balloon-1":[{"page":3,"paragraphs":["The instructions in the video for this particular lesson have been updated, please see the lesson notes below for the corrected version.","In this lesson, we will guide you through the initial setup of a new project in Unreal Engine. We will cover creating a blank project, organizing the file structure for our mini-games, creating a new level, and importing a custom 3D model to use as our primary asset.","Creating Your Unreal Engine Project","First, we need to create a new project from the Unreal Project Browser, which appears after launching the engine. Navigate to the Games section on the left-hand side and select the Blank template to start with a clean slate.","At the bottom of the window, set the project location.","Give your project a name. For this course, we will name it MiniProjects. Once configured, click the Create button to open the editor.","Organizing the Project Structure","To keep our assets organized, we will create a dedicated folder for each mini-project we build. Our"]},{"page":4,"paragraphs":["first project is a balloon popper game. Open the Content Drawer by pressing Ctrl + Space. Then, inside the main Content folder, right-click to open the context menu. Select New Folder and name it Balloon Popper. All assets related to this game will be stored here.","Creating a New Level","The default level is a large open world, which is more than we need. We will create a smaller, more suitable level for our game. From the main menu at the top-left, navigate to File > New Level.","In the pop-up window, select the Basic level template and click Create."]},{"page":5,"paragraphs":["Save the new level by pressing Ctrl + S or clicking the save icon. When the save dialog appears, navigate into the Balloon Popper folder you created earlier. Name the level Balloon Popper Level and click Save.","Now, whenever you want to work on this game, you can simply double-click this level file in the Content Drawer to open it.","Importing a 3D Model","Unreal Engine allows you to import custom 3D models. We will now import the balloon model provided in the course files. Download and extract the course assets zip file.","The following instructions have been updated, and differ from the video: In Unreal Engine 5.6 and newer, there is a known issue when importing OBJ models – the changes to the import settings have no effect on the model. To circumvent this issue, we’re providing an FBX version of the following BalloonModel.obj file. Use BalloonModel.fbx instead of the OBJ file. Note that you do not need to adjust the import settings for the FBX model; it will be sized and rotated correctly when imported with the default settings.","Inside, you will find a file named BalloonModel.obj."]},{"page":6,"paragraphs":["With your Unreal Engine project open, simply click and drag this .obj file from your computer’s file explorer into the Balloon Popper folder within the Content Drawer.","An import options window will appear. This allows you to configure how the model is brought into the engine."]},{"page":7,"paragraphs":["Adjusting Import Settings","Note that these import settings changes are not necessary if you imported the FBX version of the balloon model.","Sometimes, models created in other software need adjustments to appear correctly in Unreal Engine. For our balloon model, we need to modify two settings.","Uniform Scale: Change this value from 1 to 100. This is because the model was created using meters as its unit of measurement, whereas Unreal Engine uses centimeters. To scale it correctly, we must multiply its size by 100. Rotation: Set the rotation for the X-axis to 90 degrees. This corrects the model’s orientation.","After adjusting these settings, click Import."]},{"page":8,"paragraphs":["You will now see a new Static Mesh asset in your folder called Balloon Model. You can drag this asset from the Content Drawer into your level to see the 3D model in your game world."]},{"page":9,"paragraphs":["You have successfully set up the project, created a level, and imported the primary game asset. In the next lesson, we will explore how to make the balloon look more appealing by creating and applying a custom material."]}],"balloon-2":[{"page":10,"paragraphs":["Welcome to this lesson where we will focus on creating a custom material for our balloon. Currently, our balloon mesh has a default grey checkerboard texture, which is not ideal. We will create a new material to give it a more appropriate, rubber-like appearance.","Preparing the Scene","To begin, we will simplify our level. Since the game will feature balloons floating in the air, a floor is not necessary. You can remove it by following these steps:","1. Select the floor Static Mesh Actor in the level viewport. 2. Press the Delete key on your keyboard to remove it.","Next, drag the balloon static mesh from the Content Drawer into your level to have an object to work with."]},{"page":11,"paragraphs":["Creating a New Material","We will now create the material asset that will define the balloon’s surface properties. In Unreal Engine, it is a common practice to follow naming conventions for different asset types. This helps in identifying assets quickly within the editor.","Materials: Use the prefix M_ (e.g., M_Balloon). Blueprints: Use the prefix BP_ (e.g., BP_Balloon).","This convention makes project management more organized, especially as the number of assets grows.","To create the material, open the Content Drawer, right-click in an empty area to open the context menu, and select Material from the list."]},{"page":12,"paragraphs":["Name the new asset M_Balloon, following the naming convention.","Editing Material Properties","Double-click on your new M_Balloon material to open the Material Editor. Here, we will adjust a few key properties on the main result node to change how the material looks.","Base Color: This property defines the fundamental color of the material. Click on the color box next to Base Color and choose a reddish color suitable for a balloon. Click OK to confirm."]},{"page":13,"paragraphs":["Roughness: This value controls how matte or glossy the surface is. A value of 1 is completely rough (matte), and 0 is perfectly smooth (a mirror). To give the balloon a slightly shiny, rubbery look, set the Roughness to a low value, such as 0.3. Specular: This property affects the intensity of reflections on the surface. Increasing this value can make highlights more prominent. A value of around 0.7 works well to enhance the balloon’s reflective quality."]},{"page":14,"paragraphs":["After adjusting these properties, remember to click the Save button in the top-left corner of the Material Editor to apply your changes.","Applying the Material","With the material saved, you can return to the main level editor. There are two primary ways to apply your new material to the balloon mesh:","Using the Details Panel: Select the balloon actor in your level. In the Details panel on the right, locate the Materials section. Click the dropdown menu for the material slot and search for “M_Balloon”. Select it to apply the material."]},{"page":15,"paragraphs":["Drag and Drop: Open the Content Drawer, find your M_Balloon material, and simply click and drag it directly onto the balloon mesh in the viewport."]},{"page":16,"paragraphs":["Your balloon should now have a shiny, red appearance."]},{"page":17,"paragraphs":["Next Steps: Creating a Blueprint","While we have successfully created and applied a material, we will now delete this balloon actor from the level. The reason for this is that for our game, we need a more robust and reusable object. In the next lesson, we will create a Blueprint for our balloon.","A Blueprint allows us to combine the mesh, material, collision, and game logic into a single, self- contained asset. This makes it easy to create many instances of the balloon, and any change made to the Blueprint will automatically update all instances in the level. Blueprints are also essential for dynamically spawning objects during gameplay."]}],"balloon-3":[{"page":18,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will create a balloon Blueprint. This asset will represent our balloon in the game. We will also set up the necessary components to detect when the balloon is clicked, preparing for the functionality to increase its size in a future lesson.","Creating the Balloon Blueprint","First, we need to create the Blueprint that will define our balloon object. A Blueprint allows us to combine components, such as models and logic, into a single reusable asset. Navigate to your Content Drawer and open the Balloon folder once again, Right-click in an empty space to open the context menu, and select Blueprint Class.","For the parent class, choose Actor. An Actor is a generic object that can be placed or spawned in the world.","Name this new Blueprint BP_Balloon. The “BP_” prefix is a common naming convention in Unreal"]},{"page":19,"paragraphs":["Engine for Blueprints, which helps in organizing assets.","Configuring the Balloon Blueprint","Now that the Blueprint exists, we need to give it a visual representation by adding a 3D model and its material. Double-click the BP_Balloon Blueprint to open the Blueprint Editor. In the Components panel, click the Add button and search for Static Mesh. Select it to add a new Static Mesh component.","Rename this new component to BalloonModel for clarity.","With the BalloonModel component selected, go to the Details panel. Find the Static Mesh"]},{"page":20,"paragraphs":["property. Click the dropdown menu and search for “Balloon Model” to assign our balloon mesh.","Next, locate the Materials section for the Static Mesh. You can open the Content Drawer and drag the balloon material directly onto the material slot in the Details panel."]},{"page":21,"paragraphs":["Click Compile and then Save in the Blueprint Editor toolbar.","You can now drag several instances of the BP_Balloon from the Content Drawer into your level.","If you press Play, you will notice that the mouse cursor disappears, preventing you from interacting"]},{"page":22,"paragraphs":["with the balloons.","Enabling the Mouse Cursor with a Player Controller","To make the mouse cursor visible during gameplay, we need to use a Player Controller. A Player Controller is a special type of Blueprint that processes player input and directs it to the appropriate actors in the game. It acts as a middleman between your keyboard/mouse inputs and the game world.","In the Content Drawer, right-click and create a new Blueprint Class.","Select Player Controller as the parent class."]},{"page":23,"paragraphs":["Name it BP_BalloonPlayerController.","Open this new Blueprint. We will not add any components; instead, navigate to the Event Graph.","The Event Graph is where visual scripting logic is created. We can delete the Event Tick node, as"]},{"page":24,"paragraphs":["we do not need code running every frame.","Drag the execution pin from the Event BeginPlay node and create a Set Show Mouse Cursor node.","Ensure the checkbox on the Set Show Mouse Cursor node is enabled (set to true). This will make the mouse cursor visible when the game starts. Compile and save the Blueprint."]},{"page":25,"paragraphs":["Setting Up the Game Mode","Even after creating the Player Controller, pressing Play will show that the mouse is still hidden. This is because the game does not yet know it should use our custom Player Controller instead of the default one. We specify this using a Game Mode Blueprint.","A Game Mode defines the rules of the game, including default classes for key actors like the player character and the Player Controller. To set this up, create another Blueprint Class in the Content Drawer.","Select Game Mode Base as the parent class."]},{"page":26,"paragraphs":["Name it BP_BalloonGameMode.","Open the Game Mode Blueprint. In the Details panel, you will see a list of default classes. Find the Player Controller Class property and use the dropdown to select our BP_BalloonPlayerController. Compile and save the Game Mode."]},{"page":27,"paragraphs":["Assigning the Game Mode to the Level","The final step is to tell our level to use the custom Game Mode we just created. Return to the main editor window and open the World Settings panel. If it is not visible, you can open it from the main menu under Window > World Settings."]},{"page":28,"paragraphs":["In the World Settings panel, find the Game Mode Override property. Click the dropdown menu and select your BP_BalloonGameMode.","Now, when you press Play, the level will use your Game Mode, which in turn uses your custom Player"]},{"page":29,"paragraphs":["Controller, and your mouse cursor will be visible on the screen.","You can now move the cursor over the balloons, preparing for the next step, where we will add interaction logic."]}],"balloon-4":[{"page":30,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will implement the functionality to click on our balloons, causing them to increase in size. We will also make them “pop” by destroying the actor once they reach a certain scale.","Detecting Clicks on Actors","To begin, we need a way to detect when a player clicks on a balloon. Unreal Engine provides a specific event node for this purpose. Navigate to your BP_Balloon Blueprint and open the Event Graph. You can delete the default event nodes (Event BeginPlay, Event ActorBeginOverlap, and Event Tick) as we will not be using them.","Right-click on an empty space in the graph, search for the On Actor Clicked event, and add this node to your graph."]},{"page":31,"paragraphs":["The On Actor Clicked event triggers whenever this specific actor is clicked on in the game world.","To test this, you can drag from its execution pin and create a Print String node."]},{"page":32,"paragraphs":["Set the string to “Clicked” to see a message on the screen when the event fires.","Enabling Click Events","If you run the game and try to click on a balloon, you will notice that nothing happens. This is because, by default, click events are not enabled in the project. We must enable them in the Player Controller. To do so, open your Balloon Player Controller Blueprint. With the top-level component selected in the Components panel, go to the Details panel."]},{"page":33,"paragraphs":["Scroll down to the Mouse Interface category and check the box for Enable Click Events. Compile and save the Blueprint as usual.","Now, if you play the level again, clicking on a balloon will correctly print the “Clicked” message to the screen, confirming that our event is working."]},{"page":34,"paragraphs":["Increasing the Balloon’s Scale","With the click event functioning, we can now modify the balloon’s scale. The logic will be to get the actor’s current scale, add a small amount to it, and then set that new value as the actor’s scale. Return to the BP_Balloon Event Graph and delete the Print String node.","From the On Actor Clicked event, drag out the execution pin and create a Set Actor Scale 3D node. This node changes the scale of an actor."]},{"page":35,"paragraphs":["To get the value for the “New Scale” input, we first need the current scale.","Right-click and create a Get Actor Scale 3D node."]},{"page":36,"paragraphs":["Drag from the “Return Value” of the Get Actor Scale 3D node and create an Add node. This will allow us to add a vector to our current scale vector.","In the second input pin of the Add node, set the X, Y, and Z values to 0.2. This is the amount the scale will increase with each click. Finally, connect the output of the Add node to the “New Scale"]},{"page":37,"paragraphs":["3D” input on the Set Actor Scale 3D node.","After compiling and saving, you can play the level. Clicking on a balloon will now cause it to grow larger uniformly."]},{"page":38,"paragraphs":["Popping the Balloon","Next, we will destroy the balloon actor once its scale exceeds a certain threshold. This will simulate the balloon popping. After the Set Actor Scale 3D node, add a Branch node.","A Branch node checks a boolean condition and executes different logic based on whether the condition is true or false.","For the condition, we need to check the actor’s current scale. Add another Get Actor Scale 3D node."]},{"page":39,"paragraphs":["Right-click on the “Return Value” pin of this new node and select Split Struct Pin.","This will expose the individual X, Y, and Z float values of the scale vector.","Since we are scaling uniformly, we only need to check one axis. Drag from the “Return Value X” pin and create a Greater Equal node."]},{"page":40,"paragraphs":["Set the second value in the Greater Equal node to 2. This will be our pop threshold. Connect the boolean output of this node to the “Condition” input of the Branch node.","Finally, drag from the “True” execution pin of the Branch node and create a Destroy Actor node."]},{"page":41,"paragraphs":["This logic checks the balloon’s scale after every click. If the X-scale is greater than or equal to 2, the actor is destroyed. Otherwise, nothing happens, and the balloon awaits the next click.","If you test this in the game, you will see that now, after clicking balloons to a certain point, they will disappear."]},{"page":42,"paragraphs":["Currently, we are “hard-coding” our values directly into the nodes (e.g., 0.2 for the size increase and 2 for the pop scale). While this works for a small project, it is better practice to use variables. For your challenge, create two new float variables: one for the size increase amount and another for the pop scale. Then, replace the hard-coded numbers in your Blueprint with these new variables."]}],"balloon-5":[{"page":43,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will refine our balloon popper project by replacing hardcoded values with variables. This will provide us with greater control and flexibility over the game’s mechanics. We will also make adjustments to the game environment, such as setting the player’s starting camera position and modifying the lighting to enhance the visual mood.","Implementing Variables for Balloon Scaling","To make our balloon’s behavior more easily adjustable, we will create variables for the size increase amount and the final pop scale. First, navigate to the “My Blueprint” panel and create a new variable by clicking the plus icon.","Name this variable SizeIncreaseAmount.","In the Details panel, change the “Variable Type” from Boolean to Float. This allows us to use decimal numbers for precise control over the size increase."]},{"page":44,"paragraphs":["Compile the Blueprint.","You can now set a default value for SizeIncreaseAmount. A value of 0.1 is a good starting point."]},{"page":45,"paragraphs":["Drag the SizeIncreaseAmount variable into the event graph and select the “Get” option."]},{"page":46,"paragraphs":["Connect this node to the addition node that increases the balloon’s scale.","You may notice that the SizeIncreaseAmount variable is a Float, while the actor’s scale is a Vector. When you connect a Float to an addition node that operates on a Vector, Unreal Engine automatically adds the Float value to each axis of the Vector (X, Y, and Z). This is a convenient feature that simplifies the graph.","Next, we will create a variable for the scale at which the balloon pops. Create another new variable and name it PopScale."]},{"page":47,"paragraphs":["The default variable type is Float, which is what we need, so there is no need to change it. Compile the Blueprint and set a default value for PopScale. Let’s use 1.5.","Drag the PopScale variable into the graph, get its value, and connect it to the comparison node, replacing the hardcoded value of 2.0."]},{"page":48,"paragraphs":["After compiling and saving, you can test the game. The balloons should now inflate and pop based on the values you set in the variables. With the core logic refined, we can now focus on improving the player’s experience by adjusting the level’s environment.","Setting the Player’s Starting Position","If the camera starts in an undesirable position, you can change it by moving the Player Start actor in the level. This actor dictates where the player spawns when the game begins. Locate the Player Start actor in your level. It may be near the floor."]},{"page":49,"paragraphs":["Select it and use the move tool to reposition it. Move it up and back until it provides a good overview of the balloons."]},{"page":50,"paragraphs":["Press play to test the new starting view and fine-tune the position as needed."]},{"page":51,"paragraphs":["Modifying the Lighting","The lighting in a scene significantly affects the game’s mood. You can easily change this by adjusting the Directional Light. Select the Directional Light actor in your level.","In the Details panel, find the “Rotation” property under Transform. Adjust the rotation values to change the angle of the sun."]},{"page":52,"paragraphs":["This will alter the color of the light and the direction of shadows, allowing you to create different moods, such as a sunset scene with long shadows."]},{"page":53,"paragraphs":["Feel free to experiment with these settings to achieve a look that you find appealing. You can also consider other challenges, such as creating balloons of different colors or making some balloons that require more clicks to pop. With these final touches, our balloon popper mini-project is complete."]}],"loops-1":[{"page":55,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will begin our next mini-project, which focuses on the concept of loops. Loops are a fundamental concept in programming and game development, allowing us to repeat a specific action multiple times. Our goal will be to spawn a large number of objects using a minimal amount of visual scripting. We will start by setting up our project and learning how to spawn a single actor.","First, we need to prepare our project environment. This involves creating a new folder to keep our assets organized and a new level to work in. Open the Content Drawer and, within the main Content folder, create a new folder named Loops.","Inside the new Loops folder, create a new level by navigating to File > New Level.","We’ll be selecting the ‘Basic’ template again."]},{"page":56,"paragraphs":["Save the new level inside your Loops folder with the name LoopsLevel.","Creating a Spawnable Actor"]},{"page":57,"paragraphs":["Before we can spawn anything, we need an object to spawn. We will create a simple Blueprint Actor for this purpose. In the Loops folder, create a new Blueprint Class.","Select ‘Actor’ as the parent class.","Name this new Blueprint BP_Box."]},{"page":58,"paragraphs":["Next, open the BP_Box Blueprint editor. Inside, add a new component by clicking the ‘Add’ button. Search for and select ‘Cube’.","Compile and save the Blueprint. You can now drag an instance of BP_Box from the Content Drawer into your level to see it."]},{"page":59,"paragraphs":["The process of creating a new instance of a Blueprint in the level is called “spawning”. While we can do this manually by dragging it from the Content Drawer, our goal is to achieve this through visual scripting.","Setting Up the Game Mode","To manage the game’s logic, including spawning our boxes, we will use a Game Mode Blueprint. The Game Mode is a suitable place for this logic because it oversees the game’s rules and flow, and it exists independently of the objects we want to spawn. An individual box cannot spawn itself if it does not yet exist in the level.","Once again, create a new Blueprint Class in the Loops folder."]},{"page":60,"paragraphs":["This time, select ‘Game Mode Base’ as the parent class.","Name it BP_LoopsGameMode."]},{"page":61,"paragraphs":["To make our level use this new Game Mode, go to the ‘World Settings’ panel. If it’s not visible, you can open it from the ‘Window’ menu.","In World Settings, find the ‘GameMode Override’ option and select your BP_LoopsGameMode from the dropdown menu."]},{"page":62,"paragraphs":["Spawning an Actor with Visual Scripting","Now we can add the visual script to spawn our BP_Box when the game starts. Open the BP_LoopsGameMode Blueprint and navigate to the Event Graph.","We will use the Event BeginPlay node, which executes as soon as the game starts. You can delete the Event Tick node as we will not need it.","Drag from the execution pin of Event BeginPlay and search for the Spawn Actor From Class node."]},{"page":63,"paragraphs":["This node requires a ‘Class’ to be specified. In Unreal Engine, the term ‘Class’ is often used interchangeably with ‘Blueprint’, stemming from its C++ foundation. For our purposes, think of a Class as a Blueprint. Click the ‘Select Class’ dropdown and search for BP_Box.","If you try to compile now, you will receive an error stating that the ‘Spawn Transform’ pin must have a connection."]},{"page":64,"paragraphs":["A Transform defines an actor’s position, rotation, and scale in the world. To provide this, right-click on the graph and create a Make Transform node. This node allows you to input a Location, Rotation, and Scale.","Connect the output of the Make Transform node to the ‘Spawn Transform’ input pin on the Spawn Actor From Class node. Let’s also set a specific location. In the Make Transform node, set the Location to X = 500 and Z = 300. This will spawn the box 5 meters forward and 3 meters up.","Compile and save the Blueprint. When you press ‘Play’ in the editor, you will see a single box appear at the specified location."]},{"page":65,"paragraphs":["The Problem with Repetition","Our ultimate goal is to create a tall tower of boxes. One way to do this is by duplicating our spawning logic. We could copy and paste the Make Transform and Spawn Actor From Class nodes multiple times, adjusting the Z location for each one to stack them vertically. For example, you could chain several Spawn Actor From Class nodes together, each with a Make Transform node that has an incrementally higher Z value (e.g., 100, 200, 300).","While this works for a few boxes, it quickly becomes inefficient and messy. If you wanted to spawn 100 boxes, you would need to copy and paste these nodes 100 times(!), which is not a practical solution."]},{"page":66,"paragraphs":["To solve this problem of repetition cleanly and efficiently, we can use a loop. We will explore how to implement loops to spawn as many boxes as we want with just a few nodes in the next lesson."]}],"loops-2":[{"page":67,"paragraphs":["In the previous lesson, we created multiple objects by duplicating and connecting several nodes. In this lesson, we will explore a much more efficient method for handling repetitive tasks by compacting our logic into just a couple of nodes.","To begin, we will simplify our Event Graph by deleting all the copied `Spawn Actor` and `Make Transform` nodes, leaving only the `Event BeginPlay` node.","Introducing the For Loop Node","The primary node we will be using is the `For Loop` node. You can create this by dragging from the execution pin of the `Event BeginPlay` node and searching for “For Loop”. It is important to select the standard `For Loop` and not the `For Each Loop`, which serves a different purpose.","The `For Loop` node is a powerful tool that allows a connected sequence of nodes to execute multiple times in a row, which is ideal for tasks that need to be repeated.","Understanding the For Loop","Unlike most nodes that execute only once when triggered, the `For Loop` is designed to iterate. The number of iterations is controlled by its input pins."]},{"page":68,"paragraphs":["First Index: This determines the starting number for the loop’s counter. We will keep this at the default value of 0. Last Index: This determines the final number for the loop’s counter. The loop will execute for every whole number from the `First Index` to the `Last Index`, inclusive. For example, if `First Index` is 0 and `Last Index` is 5, the loop will run a total of six times (for indices 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5).","The `For Loop` also has several output execution pins that control the flow of logic:","Loop Body: This execution pin fires once for every iteration of the loop. Any nodes connected to this pin will be executed repeatedly. Index: This is a data pin that outputs the current iteration number. It starts at the `First Index` and increases by one with each loop until it reaches the `Last Index`. Completed: This execution pin fires only once, after the loop has finished all of its iterations.","Visualizing the Loop in Action","To better understand how the loop works, we can connect its outputs to a `Print String` node. This will let us see messages on the screen each time the loop runs. Connect the `Loop Body` execution pin to a `Print String` node. Set the `Last Index` to 5."]},{"page":69,"paragraphs":["When you run the game, you will see your message printed to the screen six times, confirming that the loop body executed for each index from 0 to 5.","To see the `Index` value change, connect the `Index` output pin directly to the `In String` input pin of the `Print String` node.","Now, when you run the game, you will see the numbers 0 through 5 printed to the screen, demonstrating how the index value increments with each iteration."]},{"page":70,"paragraphs":["As a challenge, try to use the `For Loop` node to spawn 15 cubes. You will need to set the `Last Index` appropriately to achieve this. In the next lesson, we will also address a key problem with this setup: all the cubes currently spawn at the same location. We will explore how to automatically position each new cube, for example, by stacking them on top of one another."]}],"loops-3":[{"page":71,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will explore a practical application of the For Loop node. We will build upon the previous setup to spawn multiple instances of a Blueprint actor into our level, arranging them in a dynamic and controlled manner.","Spawning Actors with a For Loop","Our initial goal is to use the For Loop to repeatedly execute the spawning logic. This avoids the need to manually place multiple actors or duplicate the spawning nodes in our graph. Begin by connecting the Spawn Actor from Class node to the Loop Body execution pin of the For Loop. This ensures the spawn node runs for each iteration of the loop.","In the Spawn Actor from Class node, set the Class to your BP_Box Blueprint.","For the Spawn Transform, create a Make Transform node. A quick way to do this is to drag from the Spawn Transform pin and select the appropriate node from the context menu."]},{"page":72,"paragraphs":["Set the Z location in the Make Transform node to 50. This will ensure the actor spawns slightly above the ground plane."]},{"page":73,"paragraphs":["Set the Last Index of the For Loop to 14 to spawn a total of 15 boxes (from index 0 to 14).","Upon running the level, you will see a single cube."]},{"page":74,"paragraphs":["However, if you inspect the World Outliner, you will find that all 15 instances of the box have been spawned.","The issue is that they are all located at the exact same position, stacked inside one another."]},{"page":75,"paragraphs":["Using the Loop Index to Offset Actors","To solve the problem of overlapping actors, we can use the Index output from the For Loop. This integer value increases with each iteration of the loop (0, 1, 2, etc.), providing a unique number we can use to calculate a different position for each actor. First, create a Make Vector node.","The output of this node will be connected to the Location pin of our Make Transform node.","To create a vertical stack, we need to modify the Z value. We can take the Index from the For Loop and multiply it by 100 using a Multiply node."]},{"page":76,"paragraphs":["Since one unit in Unreal Engine is one centimeter, this calculation will separate each box by one meter. Connect the result of the multiplication to the Z input of the Make Vector node. Note that this will add an automatic conversion node that changes the number from an integer to a float."]},{"page":77,"paragraphs":["Now, when you run the level, the boxes will be spawned on top of one another, forming a tall tower.","Incorporating Physics and Refining Placement","To make our tower of boxes more interactive, we can enable physics. This will allow the boxes to react to forces and collisions. First, it is good practice to move the Player Start actor in the level so"]},{"page":78,"paragraphs":["that you do not spawn inside the tower of cubes.","Next, open your BP_Box Blueprint. Select the cube component and, in the Details panel, find the Physics section and check the box for Simulate Physics. Compile and save the Blueprint."]},{"page":79,"paragraphs":["When you play the level now, you will notice the tower immediately collapses. This happens because the first box (at index 0) is spawned with its center at Z=0, meaning half of it is below the ground, causing an unstable physical reaction."]},{"page":80,"paragraphs":["To fix this, we must add a vertical offset. In your spawning logic, after multiplying the Index by 100, use an Add node to add 50."]},{"page":81,"paragraphs":["This will shift the entire tower up by 50 units, ensuring the first box rests securely on the ground. The correct order of operations is crucial: (Index * 100) + 50. You must multiply the index first to calculate the stack position and then add the base offset.","With this adjustment, the tower of cubes will spawn in a stable stack, ready to be interacted with. You can use the free-roaming camera to bump into the tower to cause it to fall.","The Power and Efficiency of Loops","The true power of this approach becomes evident when you need to spawn a large number of actors. By simply changing the Last Index value in the For Loop to a higher number, such as 100, you can instantly spawn 100+ boxes.","This creates a massive tower that demonstrates the dynamic and scalable nature of procedural generation using loops."]},{"page":82,"paragraphs":["Without a loop, creating such a structure would require either placing 100 actors manually in the editor or creating a cumbersome Blueprint with 100 separate Spawn Actor from Class nodes. The For Loop provides an elegant and efficient solution for repetitive tasks."]}],"bowling-1":[{"page":84,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will begin our next mini-project: a bowling game. We will focus on setting up the essential elements, including the bowling pins and the lane. This will involve importing assets, creating Blueprints and Materials, and arranging them within a new level. By the end of this lesson, you will have a complete bowling lane setup, ready for the gameplay mechanics that will be introduced later.","Project and Level Setup","First, we need to organize our project files and create a dedicated level for the game. In the Content Browser, navigate to the main `Content` folder and create a new folder named `Bowling`.","Locate the bowling pin 3D model provided in the course files and drag it into your new `Bowling` folder to import it."]},{"page":85,"paragraphs":["During the import process, ensure the settings are configured as follows: set the Uniform Scale to 1 and the Rotation to 0 for all axes. Click Import.","Next, create a new level by navigating to File > New Level and","Select the Basic template."]},{"page":86,"paragraphs":["Save this new level inside your `Bowling` folder and name it `BowlingLevel`.","Creating the Bowling Pin Blueprint","To efficiently manage multiple pins, we will create a Blueprint. This allows any changes made to the Blueprint to automatically apply to all instances of the pin in our level. In the `Bowling` folder, create a new Blueprint Class."]},{"page":87,"paragraphs":["Select Actor as the parent class and name it `BP_BowlingPin`.","Once created, open the `BP_BowlingPin` Blueprint."]},{"page":88,"paragraphs":["We’ll first add a new Static Mesh component.","Name this new component `Model`.","In the Details panel for the `Model` component, locate the Static Mesh property and assign your imported bowling pin model to it."]},{"page":89,"paragraphs":["Creating the Bowling Pin Material","The default gray checkerboard texture is not suitable for our pins, so let’s create a custom Material."]},{"page":90,"paragraphs":["In the Content Browser, create a new Material."]},{"page":91,"paragraphs":["Name it `M_BowlingPin` to keep with Unreal naming conventions.","Open the Material Editor and begin by setting the Base Color to a creamy white (Hex FFF0D1FF)."]},{"page":92,"paragraphs":["To give the pin a slightly reflective surface, set the Roughness value to approximately `0.2`.","Save the Material and return to the `BP_BowlingPin` Blueprint. Drag the `M_BowlingPin` Material from the Content Browser and drop it onto the Material slot for the `Model` component."]},{"page":93,"paragraphs":["Then, compile and save the Blueprint. You should now have a white bowling pin to work with."]},{"page":94,"paragraphs":["Arranging the Pins and Lane","With our pin Blueprint ready, we can now set up the bowling lane and position the pins. Drag the `BP_BowlingPin` from the Content Browser into the `BowlingLevel`."]},{"page":95,"paragraphs":["You can duplicate this pin by selecting it and using Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V."]},{"page":96,"paragraphs":["Arrange the pins in a standard triangular formation at one end of the play area.","You can position them precisely by adjusting their location values in the Details panel."]},{"page":97,"paragraphs":["To keep the Outliner organized, right-click in the panel and select Create Folder.","Name this new folder `Bowling Pins`. Then, drag all your bowling pin actors into this folder."]},{"page":98,"paragraphs":["Select the default floor actor in the level. Use the Scale tool to resize it into a long, narrow shape to serve as the bowling lane.","Move your `Bowling Pins` folder so the pins are positioned at the far end of the lane."]},{"page":99,"paragraphs":["Creating the Bowling Lane Material","Let’s create a glossy wood material for our lane now. Create a new Material in your `Bowling` folder."]},{"page":100,"paragraphs":["Name the material `M_BowlingLane`.","Open the Material and set the Base Color to a brownish, wood-like color (we used Hex 8A5D2DFF). Ensure it contrasts well with the white pins."]},{"page":101,"paragraphs":["To create a glossy effect, set the Roughness to `0.2` and the Specular to `0.8`.","Save the Material and return to the level. Then, select the floor actor, and in the Details panel, assign the `M_BowlingLane` Material to it."]},{"page":102,"paragraphs":["For an additional challenge, you can make the lane narrower by adjusting its Y-axis scale. A value of `0.6` is a good starting point.","Next Steps and Challenge"]},{"page":103,"paragraphs":["We have successfully set up the visual components of our bowling game. In the next lesson, we will create the bowling ball and implement the logic for moving it and launching it down the lane.","As a challenge before the next lesson, try to complete the following tasks:","Create a new Blueprint named `BP_BowlingBall` that contains a sphere mesh. Create a new Material that is glossy and purple. Apply this new material to your bowling ball. Place an instance of the `BP_BowlingBall` at the starting end of the lane."]}],"bowling-2":[{"page":104,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will create the bowling ball that our player will control. This involves creating a new Blueprint Actor, designing a custom material for its appearance, and setting up the necessary player inputs to allow for movement and throwing the ball. We will be using Unreal Engine’s Enhanced Input System to handle these actions.","Creating the Bowling Ball Blueprint","First, we need an object to represent our bowling ball. We will create this as a Blueprint Actor, which is a reusable object that can be placed in our game world. Open the Content Drawer and create a new Blueprint Class","As usual, select the parent class to be of type Actor."]},{"page":105,"paragraphs":["Name this new asset BP_BowlingBall.","Open the BP_BowlingBall Blueprint Editor. In the Components panel, add a new Sphere component. This will serve as the visual and physical representation of our ball."]},{"page":106,"paragraphs":["For consistency, it is good practice to rename the Sphere component to Model.","Designing the Material","With the shape of our ball created, let’s give it a distinct look by creating and applying a new material. In the Content Drawer, create a new Material and name it M_BowlingBall.","Drag the M_BowlingBall material onto the Material slot for the Model component within the BP_BowlingBall Blueprint."]},{"page":107,"paragraphs":["Now, let’s open the M_BowlingBall Material Editor to modify its properties. For the Base Color, choose a purple shade (we used Hex C461E5FF)."]},{"page":108,"paragraphs":["Set the Roughness value to 0 to make the ball very reflective. Also, set the Specular value to approximately 0.8. This will make the ball reflective but not a perfect mirror.","Save the material and close the editor. After compiling and saving the Blueprint, you can drag an instance of BP_BowlingBall from the Content Drawer into your level to see it."]},{"page":109,"paragraphs":["Setting Up Player Inputs","To control the ball, we need to configure player inputs. Unreal’s Enhanced Input System uses two main assets: Input Actions and an Input Mapping Context.","Input Actions represent a specific action the player can perform, such as “move” or “bowl”. An Input Mapping Context links these actions to specific physical keys or buttons.","Let’s create these assets. In the Content Drawer, create a new folder named Inputs to stay organized."]},{"page":110,"paragraphs":["Inside the Inputs folder, right-click, navigate to the Input category, and create an Input Mapping Context.","Name it BowlingInputs and open the BowlingInputs asset."]},{"page":111,"paragraphs":["Configuring Input Actions","Inside the Input Mapping Context, we will define our game’s actions and map them to keys. In the Mappings section, click the plus icon to add a new mapping.","Click the dropdown next to the new mapping and select “Input Action” under the ‘Create New Asset’ section.."]},{"page":112,"paragraphs":["Save it in your Inputs folder as IA_Bowl.","For the IA_Bowl action, click the keyboard icon and press the Spacebar to assign it.","Add another mapping for our movement. Create a new Input Action asset named IA_Move."]},{"page":113,"paragraphs":["For the IA_Move action, we need two key bindings. First, assign the Left Arrow key.","Click the plus icon next to the IA_Move mapping to add a second key. Assign the Right Arrow key to this new binding."]},{"page":114,"paragraphs":["Modifying the Move Action","Our move action needs to provide directional data. We want the Left Arrow key to represent a value of -1 and the Right Arrow key to represent +1. By default, both will return 1. For the Left Arrow key mapping under IA_Move, expand its properties and find the Modifiers section. Add a new modifier and select Negate. This will invert the input’s value, making it -1 when pressed.","Additionally, the IA_Move action needs to return a number (a float), not just a true/false value. We must change its value type. In the Content Drawer, open the IA_Move asset.","In the Details panel, change the Value Type from “Digital (bool)” to Axis 1D (float). This allows the action to output a floating-point number, which is perfect for representing movement direction."]},{"page":115,"paragraphs":["With our bowling ball created and the input system configured, we are ready to connect everything. In the next lesson, we will create a Player Controller Blueprint. This will be responsible for applying our Input Mapping Context to the game and using the input events to move and bowl the ball."]}],"bowling-3":[{"page":116,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will establish the foundational Blueprints for our game’s logic: the Player Controller and the Game Mode. These components are essential for defining the rules of the game and how the player interacts with it. We will create these Blueprints, configure them to work together, and set up the initial input handling.","Creating Core Blueprints","To begin, we need to create three new Blueprint classes within our “Bowling” folder. These will manage the game’s rules, player input, and the player’s viewpoint. Right-click in the Content Browser, select “Blueprint Class,” and choose Game Mode Base.","Name this new Blueprint BP_BowlingGameMode.","Create another Blueprint Class, this time of type Player Controller."]},{"page":117,"paragraphs":["Name it BP_BowlingPlayerController.","Finally, create one more Blueprint Class of type Pawn. This will serve as our static camera."]},{"page":118,"paragraphs":["Name it BP_BowlingCamera. The ball itself is not the player; the player is this camera observing the scene.","Configuring the Game Mode","The Game Mode Blueprint is responsible for defining the default classes used in a level. We need to configure our new Game Mode to use the Player Controller and Pawn we just created. Open the BP_BowlingGameMode Blueprint. In the Details panel, locate the “Classes” section. Then:","Set the Player Controller Class to our BP_BowlingPlayerController. Set the Default Pawn Class to our BP_BowlingCamera."]},{"page":119,"paragraphs":["Compile and save the Blueprint. Next, we must apply this Game Mode to our level. Return to the main level editor and open the World Settings panel. Under “Game Mode,” set the Game Mode Override to our BP_BowlingGameMode.","If you press “Play” now, you will notice that you can no longer move the camera."]},{"page":120,"paragraphs":["This is because the level is now using our custom `BP_BowlingCamera` pawn, which currently has no movement logic. This is the expected behavior.","Positioning the Player Camera","Let’s position our camera for a good view of the bowling lane. The camera’s spawn location is determined by the PlayerStart actor in the level. First, locate the PlayerStart actor in your level."]},{"page":121,"paragraphs":["Move and rotate it to a position that gives a clear view of the bowling ball and pins."]},{"page":122,"paragraphs":["Press “Play” again to confirm the new camera position."]},{"page":123,"paragraphs":["Setting Up Player Input","Now, we will configure the `BP_BowlingPlayerController` to listen for our defined inputs. By default, an Input Mapping Context is not active; we must enable it. Open the BP_BowlingPlayerController Blueprint and navigate to the Event Graph.","Delete the Event Tick node, as we will not need it."]},{"page":124,"paragraphs":["From the Event BeginPlay node, we will add a sequence of nodes to activate our input mapping. First, right-click and add a Get a reference to self node.","Drag from its output pin and find the Get Enhanced Input Local Player Subsystem node."]},{"page":125,"paragraphs":["From the subsystem’s output, add an Add Mapping Context node.","Connect the execution pin from Event BeginPlay to the Add Mapping Context node. Finally, in the Add Mapping Context node, select BowlingInputs from the “Mapping Context” dropdown menu."]},{"page":126,"paragraphs":["To verify that our inputs are being detected, we can add a simple test. Right-click in the Event Graph and search for the IA_Bowl event.","Connect the Started execution pin to a Print String node."]},{"page":127,"paragraphs":["Compile, save, and press “Play.” Now, when you press the spacebar, a message should appear on the screen, confirming that the input is working.","Finding the Bowling Ball","Our input logic will reside in the Player Controller, not the bowling ball. The Player Controller will find the ball in the level and then send commands to it. To do this, we first need a reference to the ball. In the “My Blueprint” panel of BP_BowlingPlayerController, create a new variable named BowlingBall."]},{"page":128,"paragraphs":["Set its “Variable Type” to BP BowlingBall (Object Reference).","Return to the Event BeginPlay graph. After the Add Mapping Context node, add a Get Actor Of Class node."]},{"page":129,"paragraphs":["Set the “Actor Class” for this node to BP_BowlingBall. This node will search the level for an actor of the specified class.","Drag the BowlingBall variable into the graph and select Set Bowling Ball."]},{"page":130,"paragraphs":["Connect the execution pin and the “Return Value” from Get Actor Of Class to the Set nodes inputs.","Now, when the game begins, our Player Controller enables the necessary inputs, finds the bowling ball in the level, and stores a reference to it in a variable. In the next lesson, we will use this setup to make the ball respond to our inputs."]}],"bowling-4":[{"page":131,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will begin detecting player inputs within our Player Controller and use them to communicate with our Bowling Ball Blueprint. This will allow us to implement the core actions for our game, starting with throwing the ball.","Creating Custom Events","First, we need to define the actions our bowling ball can perform. We will do this by creating custom events within the BP_BowlingBall Blueprint. These events can then be called from other Blueprints, such as our Player Controller.","To begin, navigate to the Event Graph of your BP_BowlingBall Blueprint and remove the default event nodes.","Right-click on the graph and create a new Custom Event."]},{"page":132,"paragraphs":["Name the event Bowl. This event will be triggered when the player presses the spacebar to throw the ball.","Create a second Custom Event and name it Move. This will handle the left and right movement of the ball."]},{"page":133,"paragraphs":["The Move event requires an input to determine the direction of movement. Select the Move node and, in its Details panel, add a new input parameter. Name this input MoveDirection and set its type to Float. This will allow us to pass a value indicating the direction (-1 for left, 1 for right, and 0 for no movement).","For initial testing, you can connect a Print String node to each event to display “Bowl” and “Move” on the screen, respectively. This helps confirm that the events are being triggered correctly before we add the full logic."]},{"page":134,"paragraphs":["Connecting Inputs to Events","With the custom events created, we can now go to our BP_BowlingPlayerController to trigger them based on player input. In the Event Graph, create the event nodes for your Input Actions: IA_Bowl and IA_Move.","For the IA_Bowl event, we will use the Started execution pin. This ensures the event fires only once when the key is first pressed, rather than continuously."]},{"page":135,"paragraphs":["Next, drag in your Bowling Ball variable. Then, pull a wire from it and call the Bowl event we created earlier.","Connect the Started pin to the Bowl node."]},{"page":136,"paragraphs":["For the IA_Move event, get the Bowling Ball variable and call the Move event.","Connect the Triggered execution pin to the Move node. Then, connect the Action Value output from the IA_Move node to the MoveDirection input on your Move node. This passes the float value from the input directly to our custom event."]},{"page":137,"paragraphs":["After compiling and playing the game, pressing the spacebar should print “Bowl” to the screen, and pressing the left or right keys should print “Move”.","Implementing the Bowling Force","Now that our communication is established, let’s add the logic to make the ball roll forward. This will involve applying physics. First, in the BP_BowlingBall Blueprint, go to the Viewport tab and select the Model component. In the Details panel, scroll down to the Physics section and check the box for Simulate Physics."]},{"page":138,"paragraphs":["Return to the Event Graph. Replace the Print String node connected to the Bowl event with an Add Force node. Ensure you select the version of the node that targets a specific component, in this case, our Model."]},{"page":139,"paragraphs":["The Add Force node has several options. Check the box for Accel Change (Acceleration Change). This makes the force disregard the object’s mass, ensuring a consistent launch speed regardless of the ball’s weight. For the Force input, we need to apply a strong push along the forward (X) axis. A value of 200,000 on the X-axis provides a good amount of force. You may need to experiment with this value to get the desired speed.","Adjusting Pin Physics","When you test the game, you will notice the ball rolls forward but the pins do not react realistically."]},{"page":140,"paragraphs":["This is because they also need to have physics enabled. Open the BP_BowlingPin Blueprint, select its Model component, and enable Simulate Physics.","Upon testing again, you may find the pins are very resistant to moving. This is because Unreal Engine calculates a default mass based on the object’s size, which can be quite high. To fix this, locate the Mass property in the Physics section of the pin’s Model. By default, it may be over 100 kg. Change this value to something much lower, such as 10 kg, to make the pins lighter and react more dynamically upon impact."]},{"page":141,"paragraphs":["With these changes, the bowling functionality is now in place. The ball launches forward and realistically knocks over the pins."]},{"page":142,"paragraphs":["In the next lesson, we will implement the logic for the Move event to allow the player to position the ball before the throw."]}],"bowling-5":[{"page":143,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will implement the logic to move our bowling ball left and right along the lane. This will allow the player to aim their shot before bowling.","Implementing Basic Movement","Our `Move` event provides a `MoveDirection` output. If we observe this value, we can see that pressing the left arrow key outputs a value of -1, and pressing the right arrow key outputs +1. We can use this directional value to directly influence the ball’s position on the Y-axis.","To achieve this, we will modify the `BP_BowlingBall` Blueprint event graph. First, remove the `Print String` node that was previously connected to our `Move` event. Next, drag from the execution pin of the `Move` event and create an Add World Offset node. This node allows us to move an object by applying a positional offset."]},{"page":144,"paragraphs":["To define the direction and magnitude of the movement, we need to create a vector. Right-click on the graph and create a Make Vector node.","Connect the `MoveDirection` output from the `Move` event to the Y input of the `Make Vector` node. This ensures our movement is restricted to the Y-axis. Finally, connect the return value of the"]},{"page":145,"paragraphs":["`Make Vector` node to the Delta Location input of the `Add World Offset` node.","After compiling and saving, you can press play. Using the left and right arrow keys will now move the ball accordingly.","However, there is a significant issue with this implementation.","The Problem of Frame Rate Dependency","The current movement logic is executed every frame that an input key is held down. This means the"]},{"page":146,"paragraphs":["ball moves 1cm for every frame. While this might seem acceptable, it creates a problem known as frame rate dependency. The speed of the ball’s movement is now tied directly to the game’s frame rate.","A player with a high-end computer running the game at a high frame rate (e.g., 200 frames per second) will experience very fast ball movement. Conversely, a player on a less powerful machine with a low frame rate (e.g., 30 frames per second) will find the ball moves much more slowly.","To ensure a consistent experience for all players, we must make our movement independent of the frame rate.","Achieving Frame Rate Independence","The solution is to use “delta time“. Delta time is the amount of time that has passed between the previous frame and the current one. By scaling our movement by delta time, we can ensure the ball moves at a consistent rate, regardless of frame rate fluctuations.","We can implement this using an input modifier. Open your BowlingInputs mapping context asset and locate the `IA_Move` input action. For the left arrow key mapping, which already has a `Negate` modifier, add a new modifier. Select Scale by Delta Time. Then, add the same Scale by Delta Time modifier to the right arrow key mapping.","Now, the output value of our input action is scaled by the time between frames, making the movement consistent. However, if you test the game now, the ball will appear to move extremely slowly. This is because we are now moving the ball at a rate of 1cm per second, which is nearly imperceptible.","Adjusting Movement Speed","To fix the slow movement, we need to introduce a speed multiplier. We can do this by creating a variable to control how fast the ball moves. Once again, return to the BP_BowlingBall Blueprint. In the “My Blueprint” panel, create a new variable named MoveSpeed."]},{"page":147,"paragraphs":["Set its type to Float.","Disconnect the output of the `Make Vector` node from the `Add World Offset` node. Drag a wire from the `Make Vector` node’s output and create a Multiply node."]},{"page":148,"paragraphs":["Drag the `MoveSpeed` variable into the graph and select “Get”.","Connect it to the other input pin of the `Multiply` node. Then, connect the output of the `Multiply` node to the Delta Location input of the `Add World Offset` node."]},{"page":149,"paragraphs":["Compile the Blueprint so that we can access the default value of the MoveSpeed variable.","With the `MoveSpeed` variable selected, set its default value in the Details panel to 100. This will correspond to a movement speed of 1 meter per second."]},{"page":150,"paragraphs":["If you run the game now, the ball will move left and right at a consistent and reasonable speed, no matter the frame rate. You can aim your shot and then bowl to hit the pins.","This completes our bowling mini-project. You have successfully implemented player controls for aiming and bowling. Feel free to expand upon this project. You could add an aiming indicator, randomize the starting position of the ball, add environments, or include sound effects. Hopefully, this project has provided a solid foundation in using input actions, creating custom events, and setting up player controllers in Unreal Engine."]}],"clicker-1":[{"page":152,"paragraphs":["Welcome to our fourth mini-project. In this lesson, we will begin creating a simple clicker game, similar to titles like Cookie Clicker. The primary focus will be on building a graphical user interface (GUI) using Unreal Engine’s UI system. We will learn how to add interactive elements like buttons and display dynamic information with text.","Project and Level Setup","Before we can start designing our UI, we need to set up a new level for our project. This provides a clean environment to work in. In the Content Drawer, create a new folder and name it ClickerGame.","Next, create a new level by navigating to File > New Level.","Like the other mini-projects, select the “Basic” template."]},{"page":153,"paragraphs":["Save this new level inside your ClickerGame folder with the name ClickerGameLevel.","Creating a Widget Blueprint","Unreal Engine’s user interface is managed through a system called UMG (Unreal Motion Graphics). The core of this system is the Widget Blueprint, which is a special type of Blueprint used to design UI layouts and functionality. Different Widget Blueprints can be used for various parts of your game, such as the main menu, the in-game HUD, or, in our case, the main screen for our clicker game.","To create our UI, first open the Content Drawer and navigate into the ClickerGame folder. Right- click on an empty space to open the context menu. Then, navigate to User Interface > Widget Blueprint."]},{"page":154,"paragraphs":["When prompted to pick a root widget, select User Widget.","Name the new asset ClickerGameUI.","Double-clicking this asset will open the Widget Blueprint Editor. This editor is where you will visually construct your user interface."]},{"page":155,"paragraphs":["Building the User Interface","Before we jump in, let’s talk a bit about this specialized editor. To begin, the Widget Blueprint Editor has a Palette panel on the left, which contains all the available UI elements you can use."]},{"page":156,"paragraphs":["The central area is the Designer, where you arrange these elements.","The Hierarchy panel shows the structure of your UI elements (though for us, it’s currently empty)."]},{"page":157,"paragraphs":["Like other components, the Details panel allows you to configure the selected element’s properties.","Let’s begin constructing our UI. The first element we need is a Canvas Panel. This acts as a container for all other UI elements, representing the screen area. Search for “Canvas Panel” in the Palette and drag it into the Designer view."]},{"page":158,"paragraphs":["You will see it appear in the Hierarchy.","Next, we need a button for the player to click. Search for “Button” in the Palette and drag it onto the Canvas Panel."]},{"page":159,"paragraphs":["A button is, of course, more useful with a label. Find the “Text” element in the Palette and drag it directly on top of the button in the Designer or Hierarchy.","This will make the text a “child” of the button, meaning they will move together. In the Hierarchy, you will also note that the button is the child of our canvas panel as well.","Configuring UI Elements","With the elements in place, we can now customize their appearance and properties using the Details panel. To keep our UI organized, select the button in the Hierarchy or Designer. In the Details panel at the top, rename it to ClickerButton."]},{"page":160,"paragraphs":["With the button still selected, you can use the white dots on its border to resize it.","You can also click and drag the button to position it. Let’s place it near the bottom-center of the screen."]},{"page":161,"paragraphs":["Next, select the Text element. In the Details panel, under the “Content” section, change the Text property to “Click”. You can also customize its appearance, such as color and font size, under the “Appearance” section. For example, we’ll make our button’s text a yellow-ish color (Hex FFCB1CFF)."]},{"page":162,"paragraphs":["We’ll also change the font settings so the size is 72. You can find this under the “Font” section."]},{"page":163,"paragraphs":["Next, select the ClickerButton again. In the Details panel, you can change its appearance. For example, under “Appearance” > “Style”, you can modify the Background Color (example Hex 222222FF)."]},{"page":164,"paragraphs":["Ultimately, the styling will be up to you – so feel free to experiment with the look!"]},{"page":165,"paragraphs":["Understanding Anchors","When you design a UI, it needs to look correct on screens of various sizes and aspect ratios. Anchors are a crucial tool for achieving this. An anchor defines which point of the parent container (in this case, the screen) a UI element should maintain its position relative to.","By default, our button’s anchor is in the top-left corner."]},{"page":166,"paragraphs":["This means that as the screen resizes, the button will always maintain its distance from that top-left point, which can cause it to go off-screen or appear misplaced.","To fix this, we want to anchor our button to the bottom-center of the screen. To change the anchor first select the ClickerButton. In the Details panel, find the Anchors property. Click the dropdown menu and select the preset that corresponds to the bottom-center position."]},{"page":167,"paragraphs":["Now, if you resize the preview screen in the Designer, you will see that the button correctly stays positioned at the bottom-center.","To prepare for the next lesson, your task is to add one more UI element. Add a new Text component to the canvas that will eventually display our score. Position this text in the middle of the screen and ensure it is properly anchored to the center."]}],"clicker-2":[{"page":168,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will finalize our user interface by adding a score display. We will then proceed to make this UI visible within our game level by creating and configuring a custom Game Mode. Finally, we will establish the foundational logic for our button, ensuring that a click event can be detected and handled within our game’s core blueprint, setting the stage for implementing the scoring mechanism.","Finalizing the UI Layout","To complete our UI, we need a text element to display the player’s score. This involves adding and configuring a Text widget within our UI Blueprint. Drag a new Text widget from the Palette onto your canvas. Be careful not to place it on the button, as this would make it a child of the button.","Position and resize the text box as desired, typically above the button."]},{"page":169,"paragraphs":["In the Details panel, change the default text to a placeholder number, such as “500”. Then, adjust the font size to make it clearly visible. We found a value of 136 works well."]},{"page":170,"paragraphs":["To center the text within its box, locate the Justification settings and select the center alignment option."]},{"page":171,"paragraphs":["To ensure the text remains centered on the screen regardless of window size, go to the Anchors dropdown and select the top-center anchor.","Finally, rename this widget to ScoreText for easy identification."]},{"page":172,"paragraphs":["Feel free to make additional tweaks, but this covers the essentials we’ll need for our UI setup.","Displaying the UI Widget in the Game","Simply creating a UI Widget Blueprint does not automatically display it when the game runs. We need to explicitly tell the level to create and show our UI. A common and effective way to manage this is through a custom Game Mode. In the Content Drawer, create a new Blueprint Class. Select Game Mode Base as the parent class."]},{"page":173,"paragraphs":["Name this new asset BP_ClickerGameMode.","To make the level use this new Game Mode, go to World Settings and assign BP_ClickerGameMode to the GameMode Override property.","Spawning the UI from the Game Mode","Now that our custom Game Mode is active, we can add logic to it that will execute when the game begins. We will use this to spawn our UI. Open the BP_ClickerGameMode Blueprint and navigate to the Event Graph. We will not need the Event Tick node, so you can delete it."]},{"page":174,"paragraphs":["Drag from the execution pin of the Event BeginPlay node and create a Create Widget node."]},{"page":175,"paragraphs":["In the Class dropdown of the Create Widget node, select your Clicker Game UI widget.","Creating the widget is only the first step; it exists in memory but is not yet visible. To display it, drag from the execution pin of the Create Widget node and add an Add to Viewport node.","Connect the Return Value output from the Create Widget node to the Target input of the Add to Viewport node. This tells the system which widget instance to add to the screen.","If you compile, save, and play the level now, you will see your button and score text appear on the"]},{"page":176,"paragraphs":["Before we move on, let’s set up a variable for our score. Back in the EventGraph, add a new variable called “Score”."]},{"page":177,"paragraphs":["In the Details panel, change its Variable Type to Integer.","We won’t use this variable right away, but this makes us ready for connecting our UI and internal game logic.","Handling Button Clicks","Our next goal is to make the button functional. The logic for what happens when the button is clicked will reside in our Game Mode, which will eventually manage the score. To achieve this, we must first make the UI elements accessible to our Blueprints and then connect the button’s click event to our Game Mode’s graph.","Making UI Elements Accessible","To reference UI elements like buttons and text in a Blueprint graph, they must be designated as variables. Open the ClickerGameUI widget and select the button. In the Details panel, check the box for Is Variable.","Do the same for the ScoreText element.","You can now switch from the Designer view to the Graph view in the top-right corner.","In the My Blueprint panel on the left, you will see variables for your button and text."]},{"page":178,"paragraphs":["Connecting the Click Event to the Game Mode","While you can create click events directly within the UI widget’s graph, it is often better practice to handle core gameplay logic, like scoring, in a central blueprint like the Game Mode. This keeps your UI focused on presentation and your Game Mode focused on logic.","Thus, back in the BP_ClickerGameMode Event Graph, we will add logic after the Add to Viewport node. First, drag from the Return Value pin of the Create Widget node (which holds our spawned UI instance) and search for Get ClickerButton. This gives us a reference to the button inside our UI.","Drag from the output pin of the Get ClickerButton node and add an Assign On Clicked node. This special node allows us to create an event that will run whenever the button is clicked."]},{"page":179,"paragraphs":["Connect the main execution flow into the Assign On Clicked node."]},{"page":180,"paragraphs":["The Assign On Clicked node has a red event pin. Drag from this pin and connect it to a Print String node. This will automatically create a new custom event in your graph that is now bound to the button’s click action.","Now, when you play the game and click the button, the “Hello” message from the Print String node will appear on the screen. This confirms that the click event is being successfully communicated from the UI widget to the Game Mode Blueprint.","With this communication established, we are ready to implement the scoring system. In the next lesson, we will create logic to modify our score each time the button is clicked – updating the text on screen to reflect the new score."]}],"clicker-3":[{"page":181,"paragraphs":["In this lesson, we will continue developing our clicker game. Previously, we set up the user interface and connected the button’s click event to our game mode. Now, we will implement the core logic: creating a score that increases with each click and displaying that score on the screen.","Implementing the Score Variable","To keep track of the player’s clicks, we need a variable to store the score. We will create this in our game mode blueprint, as it is responsible for managing the game’s state. If you didn’t do so last lesson, in your game mode blueprint, create a new variable. Name it score and set its type to Integer.","Locate the custom event that is triggered when the UI button is clicked. We will modify its logic to update the score. Instead of printing a string, we will now get the current value of our score variable, add one to it, and then set the score variable to this new value. This will require a Get Node of the Score variable, an Add node, and a Set Node.","To verify that this is working, you can temporarily use a Print String node to display the value of the score variable on the screen after it has been updated."]},{"page":182,"paragraphs":["If you run the game and click the button, you should see a number in the top-left corner of the screen that increases by one with each click.","However, the text in our UI widget remains unchanged. Our next step is to update this text.","Updating the UI with the Score","To update the score displayed in the UI, the game mode needs to communicate the new score to the UI widget. We will achieve this by creating a custom event within the UI blueprint that the game mode can call.","Open the ClickerGameUI blueprint and navigate to the Event Graph and create a new Custom Event."]},{"page":183,"paragraphs":["Name it SetScoreText.","Select the new event node and, in the Details panel, add an input parameter. Name this input Score and set its type to Integer."]},{"page":184,"paragraphs":["Drag a reference to the ScoreText variable from the My Blueprint panel into the graph. Select the Get ScoreText option.","From the ScoreText reference, drag a wire and add a Set Text node."]},{"page":185,"paragraphs":["Connect the execution pin from the SetScoreText event to the Set Text node.","The Set Text node requires a Text input, but our score is an Integer. Drag a wire from the Score input of our custom event and connect it to the In Text pin of the Set Text node. Unreal Engine will automatically add a conversion node to change the Integer into Text."]},{"page":186,"paragraphs":["With this event created, our UI now has a way to receive a score and update its display accordingly.","Connecting the Game Mode and the UI","Now we need to call the SetScoreText event from our game mode every time the score changes. To do this, we must first store a reference to the UI widget when we create it. Return to the game mode blueprint. Create a new variable and name it ClickerUI.","Set its variable type to Clicker Game UI (Object Reference)."]},{"page":187,"paragraphs":["In the Event BeginPlay logic, find the Create Widget node. Drag a wire from its Return Value pin and connect it to a Set ClickerUI node. This saves our newly created widget into the variable. To ensure this happens before you bind the button’s click event, you’ll also want to connect the execution flow pins so Add to Viewport goes into the new Set ClickerUI node first.","You can now get the Clicker Button from the ClickerUI variable instead of the Create Widget node’s return value. This can be, in turn, fed into the On Clicked binding node.","Now, go to the custom event that handles the button click. After the score is incremented, remove the Print String node. Get the ClickerUI variable, drag a wire from it, and call the SetScoreText event."]},{"page":188,"paragraphs":["Connect the updated score variable to the Score input pin of the SetScoreText node. Make sure to connect the execution flow as well.","If you compile, save, and play the game now, clicking the button will increase the score and update the text on the screen in real-time."]},{"page":189,"paragraphs":["Initializing the Score Display","You may notice that when the game starts, the score text displays its default value from the designer, not “0”."]},{"page":190,"paragraphs":["We can easily fix this by setting the initial text when the UI is first created. In the ClickerGameUI blueprint’s Event Graph, add an Event Construct node. This event runs once when the widget is created, similar to Event BeginPlay for actors."]},{"page":191,"paragraphs":["From the Event Construct node, call the SetScoreText event.","Set the Score input for this node to 0."]},{"page":192,"paragraphs":["Now, when the game begins, the UI will immediately call SetScoreText to display “0” before the player makes their first click.","You have now successfully implemented a complete scoring system for the clicker game. Feel free to expand upon this project. As a challenge, you could try adding an auto-clicker feature or applying these UI principles to other projects from this course, such as adding a score counter to the balloon popper game."]}]};
